<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trần Thái Tông</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1218">1218</a>–<a href="/years/1277">1277</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Trần Cảnh, Tran Thai Tong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trần Thái Tông (1218–1277), born Trần Cảnh, was the first emperor of the Trần dynasty of Đại Việt. Married as a child to the young empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-chieu-hoang">Lý Chiêu Hoàng</a> in 1225, he received the throne when she abdicated, ending the Lý dynasty, while his uncle Trần Thủ Độ held real power. He repelled the first Mongol invasion in 1258 and then abdicated in favour of his son.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Trần Cảnh, also called Trần Nhật Cảnh, was born on 17 July 1218 in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nam-dinh">Nam Định</a> province, in the last years of the Lý dynasty. The ancestors of the Trần clan had come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> under Trần Kính, and the Chinese writer Zhou Mi claimed that his real name was Hsieh Sheng-ch'ing, a man from Fujian. His father, Trần Thừa, was a senior official of the Lý court, and his rise to the throne was mostly the work of his uncle Trần Thủ Độ, then commander of the Lý capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He reigned for 33 years under the era names Kiến Trung, Thiên Ứng Chính Bình and Nguyên Phong. In 1237, with no son after the early death of Crown Prince Trần Trịnh, he followed the advice of Trần Thủ Độ and made Princess Thuận Thiên, the wife of his elder brother Trần Liễu, then three months pregnant, his empress, demoting Chiêu Hoàng. Trần Liễu rose in revolt at the Cai River, and the emperor, ashamed, withdrew to Yên Tử Mountain until the monk Đạo Viên and Trần Thủ Độ persuaded him to return. When Trần Liễu surrendered two weeks later, the emperor shielded him with his body from execution and granted him lands.</p><p>In the autumn of 1257 the Mongol general Uriyangkhadai sent three letters demanding passage to attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, and when the envoys were imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thang Long</a> he invaded. The emperor led his army from an elephant, but Aju ordered his troops to shoot at the animals' feet, the elephants panicked and the army was beaten at No Nguyen and again at the Phu Lo bridge, so the court evacuated the capital. After the Mongol departure from Thang Long, he agreed to send tribute to the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> every three years. In 1258 he abdicated in favour of his son, Crown Prince Trần Hoảng.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He lived 19 more years as retired emperor and died on 5 May 1277. Learned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he wrote many works on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a>, few of which survive; the best known, the Khóa Hư Lục or Instructions on Emptiness, a collection of sermons and essays, still influences Vietnamese Buddhism. One of his daughters, Princess An Tư, married Prince Toghan, ninth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trần Thái Tông” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1225 the young empress Ly Chieu Hoang was married to <b>Tran Canh</b> and abdicated in his favour, founding the Tran dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/ly-dynasty">Ly dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1225 Tran Thu Do arranged the marriage of the child empress Ly Chieu Hoang to his nephew <b>Tran Canh</b>, who became emperor Tran Thai Tong, and the Tran ruled Dai Viet from Thang Long.</blockquote><a href="/state/tran-dynasty">Tran dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nam Định, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.25,106.25&amp;year=1256" aria-label="Nam Định on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7414 1414 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Trần Thái Tông"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7864 1684h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7864 1684h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.25,106.25&amp;year=1256">What was here in 1256? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
